WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:15.330
Hi! English verbs change their form to show time. This is called tense. Today we'll review the tenses that come up most in Primary 4, and learn to use the time words in a sentence to choose the answer.

2
00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:26.778
The simple present tells us about habits. It often comes with words like every, usually and always. With he, she, it or one person, add s to the verb: washes.

3
00:00:26.928 --> 00:00:38.754
The present continuous tells us what is happening right now: am, is or are plus the verb with ing. Words like Look!, Listen! and now are clues.

4
00:00:38.904 --> 00:00:47.370
The simple past is for finished actions. Look for yesterday, last week or two days ago, and use the past form of the verb.

5
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:55.650
The simple future is for things that haven't happened yet: will plus the base verb, with words like tomorrow and next Friday.

6
00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:07.914
The past continuous, was or were plus ing, is for an action going on in the past. Often a short action interrupts it: Mother was cooking when the lights went out.

7
00:01:08.064 --> 00:01:17.658
The present perfect, has or have plus the past participle, is for something done that matters now. It often comes with already, just, since and for.

8
00:01:17.808 --> 00:01:28.002
The past perfect, had plus the past participle, is for the earlier of two past actions. The film started first, and then we arrived.

9
00:01:28.152 --> 00:01:34.986
Watch out! The present perfect can't be used with finished-time words like yesterday or last week!

10
00:01:35.136 --> 00:01:39.162
With yesterday, we say: Yesterday I finished it.

11
00:01:39.312 --> 00:01:45.690
But I have already finished it is correct, because already is not a finished time.

12
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:57.906
Your turn! My brother usually, ___, his homework before dinner. Finish, finishes or finished? Pause and think.

13
00:01:58.056 --> 00:02:09.522
Finishes. Usually tells us it is a habit, so we use the simple present, and my brother is one person, so we add es. Did you get it right?

14
00:02:09.672 --> 00:02:20.082
Another one! Ben, ___, when the phone rang. Use the correct form of read. Pause and think.

15
00:02:20.232 --> 00:02:28.266
Was reading. He was in the middle of reading when the phone rang and interrupted him. Did you get it right?

16
00:02:28.416 --> 00:02:40.002
Last one! Next Monday, our class, ___, to the museum. Use the correct form of go. Pause and think.

17
00:02:40.152 --> 00:02:48.306
Will go. Next Monday hasn't come yet, so we use the simple future. Did you get it right?

18
00:02:48.456 --> 00:03:00.426
Remember: every and usually take the simple present. Look and now take the present continuous. Yesterday and ago take the simple past. Next and tomorrow take will.

19
00:03:00.576 --> 00:03:11.178
An action interrupted in the past takes was or were plus ing. Already and since take has or have. For two past actions, the earlier one takes had.

20
00:03:11.328 --> 00:03:16.314
When you do a question, circle the time word first!

